window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_electrical_engineering_core_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_electrical_engineering_core_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_electrical_engineering_core_en"][7]={"university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M7-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M7-L1 – Electric Power Supply and Power Balances<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M7 – Bachelor: Electric Power Engineering<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">AC power and\nfrequency response<\/a>. You should be familiar with RMS values and\ncomplex phasors.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Identify generation,\ntransmission, distribution and loads in a single-line diagram, establish\na power balance and use it to determine energy transfer and\nefficiency.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Power on its way to the load<\/h2>\n<p>An electricity supply system connects conversion, transport and use.\nA generator or a converter-coupled system feeds in electrical power. A\ntransformer couples voltage levels. A transmission line connects major\nsupply nodes; the distribution network delivers power to loads. Storage\nand local generation can temporarily reverse the direction of power\nflow. Whether a node is consuming or supplying power at a given time\nfollows from the balance at its connection point.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>single-line diagram<\/strong> shows electrical connections\nand the main components, using one line per connection. In a three-phase\nnetwork the line can represent three conductors. The drawing establishes\nwhich elements are directly connected. Phase sequence, individual\nconductor currents and physical cable routing must be specified\nseparately when necessary.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_018_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Single-line diagram of a modelled supply system. From the feed-in point, a voltage conversion stage, transmission line and distribution network lead to the load. Power quantities suitable for balancing are marked at the interfaces.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: From source to load, the modelled active power falls\nfrom 1.50 to 1.20 kW; intermediate sections account for losses of 0.10\nand 0.20 kW.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Balances at nodes and interfaces<\/h2>\n<p>Active power\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>P<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin watts is energy transfer per unit time averaged over one period. For\nsinusoidal quantities in steady state, with RMS phasors\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><munder><mi>U<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\underline U<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><munder><mi>I<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\underline I<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe following holds per phase:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi>S<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mo>=<\/mo><munder><mi>U<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><munder><mi>I<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><munder><mi>S<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>P<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>Q<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\underline S=\\underline U\\,\\underline I^*=P+jQ,\n\\qquad\n|\\underline S|=\\sqrt{P^2+Q^2}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Here\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><munder><mi>I<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\underline I<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\npoints into the load under consideration. Positive active and reactive\npower represent absorption under this passive sign convention. An\ninductive load current lags the voltage and gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis specified in var and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><munder><mi>S<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\underline S|<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin VA. Reactive power describes periodic energy exchange in the\nsinusoidal model. It can increase RMS current and thus line losses.<\/p>\n<p>For our energy balance, we use the <strong>magnitudes of active\npower<\/strong> specified in the direction of flow. In a steady-state\nsection without additional feed-in,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>η<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_{\\mathrm{in}}=P_{\\mathrm{out}}+P_{\\mathrm{loss}},\n\\qquad\n\\eta=\\frac{P_{\\mathrm{out}}}{P_{\\mathrm{in}}}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Efficiency is dimensionless and requires\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_{\\mathrm{in}}&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf stored energy within the boundary also changes,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{d}W_{\\mathrm{stored}}/\\mathrm{d}t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis added to the right-hand side of the instantaneous energy balance. For\nconstant average active power,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>W<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W=P\\,\\Delta t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor load profiles, add the energy contributions of the time\nintervals.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example with three balance boundaries<\/h2>\n<p>In a model, a source continuously delivers\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.50\\,\\mathrm{kW}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat the electrical grid connection. After conversion and transmission,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.40<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.40\\,\\mathrm{kW}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nremains, and the user receives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.20\\,\\mathrm{kW}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nafter distribution.<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-15\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-21\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-21\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-21\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-21\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Section<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Input<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Output<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Losses<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Efficiency<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Conversion plus transmission<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.50\\,\\mathrm{kW}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.40<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.40\\,\\mathrm{kW}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.10\\,\\mathrm{kW}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>93.33<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">93.33\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Distribution<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.40<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.40\\,\\mathrm{kW}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.20\\,\\mathrm{kW}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.20\\,\\mathrm{kW}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>85.71<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">85.71\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Entire electrical path<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.50\\,\\mathrm{kW}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.20\\,\\mathrm{kW}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.30<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.30\\,\\mathrm{kW}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>80.00<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">80.00\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The product of the two section efficiencies is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.80<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.80<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nbecause the intermediate power cancels when multiplying the ratios. In\nfour hours, the user receives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4.8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4.8\\,\\mathrm{kWh}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe source supplies\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6.0\\,\\mathrm{kWh}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the path dissipates\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.2\\,\\mathrm{kWh}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nas lost energy. The efficiency calculation starts at the electrical\nsource terminals. Primary energy used for generation belongs within a\ncorrespondingly larger balance boundary.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations<\/h2>\n<p>A single-line diagram and an active power balance answer different\nquestions. The diagram shows the connections. The balance determines how\nmuch average energy per unit time crosses the boundary. Voltage drop and\ncurrent limits additionally require voltages, impedances and load\nbehaviour. In unbalanced three-phase systems, examine the phases\nindividually. For highly distorted waveforms, include the relevant\nharmonics or use a time-domain calculation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises with solutions<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A load draws\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.60<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.60\\,\\mathrm{kW}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor two hours and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.50\\,\\mathrm{kW}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the following hour. What are the energy and average power?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>W<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.60<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>1.50<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.70<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W=2\\cdot0.60+1\\cdot1.50=2.70\\,\\mathrm{kWh}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe average power over three hours is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.90<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.90\\,\\mathrm{kW}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A phase has\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>300<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P=300\\,\\mathrm W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>400<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">v<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q=400\\,\\mathrm{var}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWhat are the apparent power, power factor and current at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">100\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><munder><mi>S<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>500<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\underline S|=500\\,\\mathrm{VA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><munder><mi>S<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.60<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P/|\\underline S|=0.60<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=5\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA resistive phase at the same voltage and active power requires\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Further reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Three-phase\nsystems, star and delta<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-061-introduction-to-electric-power-systems-spring-2011/d79822ef5fbdb21b1c44e1b3b8282453_MIT6_061S11_ch1.pdf\">J.\nL. Kirtley, MIT 6.061, Review of Network Theory<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Chapter 1,\npp. 1–2: network topology, nodes and Kirchhoff laws; partial scope. ·\nLast accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.energy.gov/sites/default/files/2023-11/FINAL_CESER%20Electricity%20Grid%20Backgrounder_508.pdf\">U.S.\nDepartment of Energy, Electric Transmission and Distribution<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · P. 1,\nExhibit 1 and Substations: generation, transmission and distribution\nstructure using the US example. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M7-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M7-L2 – Three-Phase Systems, Star and Delta<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M7 – Bachelor: Electric Power Engineering<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Sinusoidal AC\nand complex impedance<\/a> and <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Power\nbalances<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish phase and line\nquantities in a balanced three-phase model, derive the star and delta\nrelationships and calculate total power.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Three phases with fixed phase relationships<\/h2>\n<p>A balanced three-phase system has three voltage phasors of equal\nmagnitude, separated by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>120<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">120^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor the sequence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a,b,c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe use as an example\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><munder><mi>U<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>∠<\/mi><msup><mn>0<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\underline U_{aN}=U_\\mathrm{ph}\\angle 0^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><munder><mi>U<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>∠<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mn>120<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\underline U_{bN}=U_\\mathrm{ph}\\angle -120^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><munder><mi>U<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>∠<\/mi><msup><mn>120<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\underline U_{cN}=U_\\mathrm{ph}\\angle 120^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAll magnitudes are RMS values. The phase sequence determines, for\nexample, the rotation direction of a resulting rotating field. The\nchoice of angular reference does not change any voltage magnitude.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_019_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Phasor diagram of a balanced system with three phase voltages separated by 120 degrees. The arrow from the tip of phasor b to the tip of a is the line-to-line voltage Uab.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: Three equal phase voltages are separated by 120°;\nthe line voltage between a and b is the difference between two\nphase-voltage phasors.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>In <strong>star (wye)<\/strong>, each load branch lies between a line\nconductor and the common star point. The line-to-line voltage is a\ndifference. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><munder><mi>U<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><munder><mi>U<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><munder><mi>U<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mn>3<\/mn><\/msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>∠<\/mi><msup><mn>30<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\underline U_{ab}=\\underline U_{aN}-\\underline U_{bN}\n  =\\sqrt{3}\\,U_\\mathrm{ph}\\angle 30^\\circ,\n\\qquad I_\\mathrm{L}=I_\\mathrm{ph}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The line current flows directly through the branch. With three\nidentical impedances, the currents separated by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>120<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">120^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsum to zero. Any neutral conductor present carries no current in this\nideally balanced sinusoidal case. With an unbalanced load, neutral\ncurrent must be determined from the individual currents.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Delta and the power relationship<\/h2>\n<p>In <strong>delta<\/strong>, each branch lies between two line\nconductors. Therefore\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_\\mathrm{ph}=U_\\mathrm{LL}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nDefining delta currents around the loop, we obtain, for example,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><munder><mi>I<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><munder><mi>I<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><munder><mi>I<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\underline I_a=\\underline I_{ab}-\\underline I_{ca}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBecause the two phase currents have equal magnitude and are separated by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>120<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">120^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mn>3<\/mn><\/msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_\\mathrm L=\\sqrt{3}\\,I_\\mathrm{ph}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe quantity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_\\mathrm{LL}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenotes the magnitude of a line-to-line voltage, and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_\\mathrm L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe magnitude of a line current.<\/p>\n<p>For three identical branches, both connections satisfy<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>P<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>φ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mn>3<\/mn><\/msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L<\/mi><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>φ<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>Q<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mn>3<\/mn><\/msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L<\/mi><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>φ<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nP &amp;= 3 U_\\mathrm{ph} I_\\mathrm{ph}\\cos\\varphi\n   = \\sqrt{3}\\, U_\\mathrm{LL}I_\\mathrm L\\cos\\varphi,\\\\\nQ &amp;= \\sqrt{3}\\, U_\\mathrm{LL}I_\\mathrm L\\sin\\varphi .\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The angle\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>φ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varphi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the angle of the <strong>phase voltage relative to its corresponding\nphase current<\/strong>, equal to the argument of the phase impedance\nunder the passive sign convention. When using line quantities to\ndetermine the angle, the additional geometrical phase shift must be\naccounted for.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example with identical resistors<\/h2>\n<p>An ideal three-phase connection supplies\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>120<\/mn><msqrt><mn>3<\/mn><\/msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>207.85<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_\\mathrm{LL}=120\\sqrt{3}\\,\\mathrm V\\approx207.85\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThree resistive branches each have\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>24<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=24\\,\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>In star, each branch has\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>120<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">120\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>120<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>800<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P=3\\cdot120\\cdot5=1\\,800\\,\\mathrm W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn delta, each branch has\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>120<\/mn><msqrt><mn>3<\/mn><\/msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">120\\sqrt3\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand carries\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5<\/mn><msqrt><mn>3<\/mn><\/msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5\\sqrt3\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nEach line conductor then carries\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>15<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">15\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>400<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P=5\\,400\\,\\mathrm W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe threefold total power follows from the square of the phase voltage,\nwhich is larger by a factor of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msqrt><mn>3<\/mn><\/msqrt><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nacross the same resistances. If the delta connection is to absorb the\nsame power as the star connection at the same supply, each of its\nresistances must be\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>72<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">72\\,\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limitations and checks<\/h2>\n<p>The relationships involving\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msqrt><mn>3<\/mn><\/msqrt><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor magnitudes assume balance and a sinusoidal fundamental here. For\nunbalanced branches, phasor differences and Kirchhoff’s laws still\napply. Power is then summed from the individual complex powers at the\nterminals. If the star point of an unbalanced star load is floating, its\npotential must also be calculated in the model. If several harmonics are\npresent, currents at the third harmonic and its integer multiples in\nparticular can add in the neutral conductor when they are in phase.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises with solutions<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A balanced star load has\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><munder><mi>Z<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>24<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><mn>18<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\underline Z_\\mathrm{ph}=(24+j18)\\,\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>120<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_\\mathrm{ph}=120\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nCalculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_\\mathrm L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>P<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>Z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>30<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|Z|=30\\,\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_\\mathrm L=4\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>φ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos\\varphi=0.8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>φ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin\\varphi=0.6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngive\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>152<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P=1\\,152\\,\\mathrm W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>864<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">v<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q=864\\,\\mathrm{var}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The star point of the same load is ideally connected to the source\nstar point. What is its neutral current?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAll phase currents have the same RMS magnitude and are separated\npairwise by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>120<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">120^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe three phasors sum vectorially to zero.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Further reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Transformers\nand voltage conversion<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-061-introduction-to-electric-power-systems-spring-2011/c6393a58319200a5344752de0cf47ec4_MIT6_061S11_ch3.pdf\">J.\nL. Kirtley, MIT 6.061, Polyphase Networks<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Chapter 3, Three Phase\nSystems and Line-Line Voltages, printed pp. 3–7; 120-degree phases,\nsqrt(3) ratio and wye/delta. Pages 1 and 7 visually checked. · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M7-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M7-L3 – Transformers and Voltage Conversion<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M7 – Bachelor: Electric Power Engineering<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Three-phase\nsystems, star and delta<\/a> and <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Induction and\nelectromagnetic energy<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calculate a voltage\ntransformation ratio and a referred load impedance, define current\ndirections unambiguously and distinguish ideal transformation from\nlosses and voltage drop.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Two windings with coupled flux<\/h2>\n<p>A transformer magnetically couples two windings. In the ideal model,\nthe same time-varying magnetic flux\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Phi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\npasses through every turn of both windings. The law of induction relates\nthe induced voltage to the rate of change of flux linkage. With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nturns and consistent polarity, the following holds for sinusoidal RMS\nphasors:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mfrac><msub><munder><mi>U<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><msub><munder><mi>U<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=\\frac{N_1}{N_2},\n\\qquad\n\\frac{\\underline U_1}{\\underline U_2}=a.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The voltages are measured from the correspondingly dotted terminals\nto the respective other terminals. The primary current\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><munder><mi>I<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\underline I_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\npoints into the primary dotted terminal. The secondary current\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><munder><mi>I<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\underline I_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis defined here as the current delivered to the load, pointing away from\nthe secondary dotted terminal. With this orientation, the ideal model\ngives<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><munder><mi>I<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msub><munder><mi>I<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mi>a<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><munder><mi>U<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><msubsup><munder><mi>I<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mn>1<\/mn><mo>*<\/mo><\/msubsup><mo>=<\/mo><msub><munder><mi>U<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><msubsup><munder><mi>I<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mn>2<\/mn><mo>*<\/mo><\/msubsup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\underline I_1=\\frac{\\underline I_2}{a},\n\\qquad\n\\underline U_1\\underline I_1^*\n=\\underline U_2\\underline I_2^*.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>If both currents are defined as entering the dotted terminals, the\nsign of the current relationship changes. Equality of the transferred\ncomplex power holds under the respective input and output conventions.\nFor a three-phase transformer, the transformation relationships\ninitially apply to each winding phase. The line voltage ratio and phase\nshift additionally depend on the connections on both sides.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Referring impedance to the primary side<\/h2>\n<p>From\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><munder><mi>U<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L<\/mi><\/msub><msub><munder><mi>I<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\underline U_2=Z_\\mathrm L\\underline I_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe load as seen from the primary circuit is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msubsup><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msub><munder><mi>U<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><msub><munder><mi>I<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>a<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L<\/mi><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z&#39;_\\mathrm L=\\frac{\\underline U_1}{\\underline I_1}\n            =a^2Z_\\mathrm L .<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The relationship also holds for complex loads with linear coupling.\nIt scales both the magnitude and the\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>X<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R/X<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncomponents together. An impedance referred to the primary side is an\nelectrical equivalent model. Physically, the load remains on the\nsecondary side. In line models, the conversion allows components\nspecified at different voltage levels to be summed on a common side.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example of output power and losses<\/h2>\n<p>We use an imagined single-phase source of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>240<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">240\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand an ideal transformer with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA resistor of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>12<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">12\\,\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis connected to the secondary side. This gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>60<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_2=60\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_2=5\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.25<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_1=1.25\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe useful power is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>300<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">300\\,\\mathrm W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nOn the primary side,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msubsup><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>4<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>192<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z&#39;_\\mathrm L=4^2\\cdot12\\,\\Omega=192\\,\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe independent check\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>240<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>192<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.25<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">240\\,\\mathrm V/192\\,\\Omega=1.25\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives the same input current.<\/p>\n<p>A simplified loss budget adds\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>12<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">12\\,\\mathrm W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor the core and winding at the same output operating point. Assuming\nthat appropriate source adjustment keeps the output power at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>300<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">300\\,\\mathrm W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe input power is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>312<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">312\\,\\mathrm W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the efficiency is approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>96.15<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">96.15\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis <strong>power balance<\/strong> determines efficiency. To calculate\nvoltage drop under load, the components of the equivalent circuit must\nbe known.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_135_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Circuit diagram of an ideal transformer with current directions, dot convention and a twelve-ohm load; referred input impedance of 192 ohms.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: For an ideal transformer with a 4:1 turns ratio, a\n12 Ω load appears as 192 Ω at the input; the separate loss budget is not\npart of the ideal model.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>The windings are represented symbolically; the eight and two arcs\nmerely illustrate the 4:1 ratio and not actual numbers of turns. Both\nvoltages are referenced to the dotted winding terminal. Ideal power\ntransfer and the separately assumed loss budget are different models at\nthe same output operating point.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A more detailed equivalent circuit<\/h2>\n<p>Winding resistances describe current-dependent copper losses. Leakage\nreactances represent flux components that couple to only one winding\neach. A parallel magnetising branch accounts for the establishment of\nflux. In a simplified sinusoidal model, a parallel loss resistance\nrepresents core losses. Voltage deviation under load therefore also\ndepends on the phase of the current. Equivalent circuit parameters may\nchange with frequency and operating conditions.<\/p>\n<p>The ideal model neglects core saturation, frequency dependence and\ndynamic switching-on transients. Voltage transformation relies on\ntime-varying flux. At constant flux, the induced voltage is zero. In a\nreal system, a constant applied winding voltage can initially cause a\nflux change up to saturation. A time-dependent model is necessary to\ndescribe this process.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises with solutions<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A load\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z_\\mathrm L=(6+j2)\\,\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis connected to an ideal transformer with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWhat does the source see?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msubsup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>54<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><mn>18<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z&#39;_\\mathrm L=9(6+j2)\\,\\Omega=(54+j18)\\,\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe impedance angle remains unchanged.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A simplified model delivers\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>450<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">450\\,\\mathrm W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof useful power with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>15<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">15\\,\\mathrm W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof losses. What is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>η<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>η<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>450<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>450<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>15<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.9677<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta=450/(450+15)\\approx0.9677<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTo determine the actual voltage ratio, the load current and equivalent\ncircuit parameters are required.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Further reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Overview of\nelectrical machines<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/15-6-transformers\">OpenStax,\nUniversity Physics Volume 2, Transformers<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Section 15.6: voltage,\ncurrent and turns ratio, and transmission losses (basic model). · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-061-introduction-to-electric-power-systems-spring-2011/c6393a58319200a5344752de0cf47ec4_MIT6_061S11_ch3.pdf\">J.\nL. Kirtley, MIT 6.061, Polyphase Networks<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Chapter 3, pp. 12–13:\nideal turns ratio and three-phase transformers. · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M7-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M7-L4 – Overview of Electrical Machines<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M7 – Bachelor: Electric Power Engineering<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Induction and\nelectromagnetic energy<\/a> and <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Three-phase\nsystems, star and delta<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish rotating magnetic\nfields, synchronous speed and slip, convert torque into mechanical\npower, and account for the losses at a motor operating point.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From the electrical to the mechanical side<\/h2>\n<p>An electrical machine couples electrical quantities and motion\nthrough a magnetic field. In <strong>motor operation<\/strong>, the\nmachine absorbs active electrical power and delivers shaft power. In\n<strong>generator operation<\/strong>, it absorbs mechanical shaft power\nand delivers active electrical power. The same physical principle may\nallow both directions of conversion. The direction depends on the\ntorques, connection conditions and operating point.<\/p>\n<p>The electromagnetic forces acting around the circumference produce a\ntorque on the rotor. Conversely, the flux linkage of a moving winding or\nmagnetic field changes. The resulting induced voltage influences the\ncurrent. The simple model of a DC machine expresses this coupling\nthrough the back EMF\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/msub><mi>ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E=k_\\mathrm{e}\\omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand electromagnetic torque\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/msub><mi>I<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_\\mathrm{e}=k_\\mathrm{t}I<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat constant excitation flux. The back EMF increases with rotational\nspeed. Mechanical losses may cause the torque at the air gap and the\nusable shaft torque to differ.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Rotating magnetic fields, pole pairs and slip<\/h2>\n<p>A stator system that is symmetrical in space and time, with\nsinusoidal currents, produces a rotating magnetic field in the ideal\nfundamental-component model. With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\npole pairs, one electrical period rotates the field mechanically by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrevolutions. At frequency\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin hertz, therefore,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/msub><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>60<\/mn><mi>p<\/mi><\/mfrac><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mfrac><mi>f<\/mi><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{n_\\mathrm{s}}{1\\,\\mathrm{min}^{-1}}\n=\\frac{60}{p}\\,\\frac{f}{1\\,\\mathrm{Hz}}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Four poles give two pole pairs. A synchronous machine rotates at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=n_\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin synchronous steady-state operation. There may be a constant angle\nbetween the rotor and stator fields, related to the torque\nexchanged.<\/p>\n<p>In an ordinary induction machine operating as a motor, the rotating\nfield moves faster than the rotor. The relative motion induces rotor\ncurrents whose interaction with the field produces torque. The relative\ndifference in rotational speed is called <strong>slip<\/strong>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/msub><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=\\frac{n_\\mathrm{s}-n}{n_\\mathrm{s}}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>At the synchronous point, steady-state induction in the\nshort-circuited rotor due to the rotating stator field vanishes in this\nidealised model. When the machine is driven above synchronous speed,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s&lt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nUnder suitable electrical conditions, this operating point allows\ngenerator operation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example: shaft power of an induction machine<\/h2>\n<p>A model motor with four poles operates at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f=50\\,\\mathrm{Hz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>440<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=1\\,440\\,\\mathrm{min}^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>500<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_\\mathrm{s}=1\\,500\\,\\mathrm{min}^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=4\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe shaft drives a load with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_\\mathrm{sh}=12\\,\\mathrm{Nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe angular velocity and shaft power are<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>440<\/mn><\/mrow><mn>60<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>150.80<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.810<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega=\\frac{2\\pi\\cdot1\\,440}{60}\\approx150.80\\,\\mathrm{rad/s},\n\\qquad\nP_\\mathrm{sh}=T_\\mathrm{sh}\\omega\\approx1.810\\,\\mathrm{kW}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>If the active electrical input power is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2.10\\,\\mathrm{kW}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe motor efficiency is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>η<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>86.2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta=P_\\mathrm{sh}/P_\\mathrm{el}\\approx86.2\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe total difference is approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.290<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.290\\,\\mathrm{kW}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSeparating this into copper, core, friction and ventilation losses\nrequires further model parameters. Slip and overall efficiency are\ndifferent dimensionless quantities.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_136_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two speed bars for the rotating field and rotor, plus a power bar showing shaft power and total loss.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: A synchronous speed of 1500 rpm and a rotor speed of\n1440 rpm give 4% slip; a separate power bar shows shaft output and total\nloss.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>The two groups of bars use separate scales. The speed difference is\nfour per cent of synchronous speed. The orange portion of the electrical\npower represents total loss and cannot be determined from slip\nalone.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Scope of the model<\/h2>\n<p>The speed formula is based on the fundamental component of the field.\nWinding arrangement, harmonics, magnetisation state and supply may\ninfluence the torque. The product of angular velocity and shaft torque\ngives the instantaneous mechanical power at the port considered, even in\ndynamic operation. A dynamic balance must also account for changes in\nstored kinetic and magnetic energy. The steady-state difference balance\ngives only the total loss at the operating point considered.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises with solutions<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A six-pole machine operates at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">50\\,\\mathrm{Hz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>960<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">960\\,\\mathrm{min}^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWhat are its synchronous speed and slip?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>000<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_\\mathrm{s}=1\\,000\\,\\mathrm{min}^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>000<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>960<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>000<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=(1\\,000-960)/1\\,000=4\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A motor absorbs\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>250<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,250\\,\\mathrm W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand has total losses of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>150<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">150\\,\\mathrm W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFind its efficiency and shaft torque at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>200<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,200\\,\\mathrm{min}^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_\\mathrm{sh}=1\\,100\\,\\mathrm W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nhence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>η<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>88<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta=88\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>40<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega=40\\pi\\,\\mathrm{rad/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthis gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>100<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>40<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>8.75<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_\\mathrm{sh}=1\\,100/(40\\pi)\\,\\mathrm{Nm}\\approx8.75\\,\\mathrm{Nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Further study:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Lines, loads\nand simplified network models<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-061-introduction-to-electric-power-systems-spring-2011/pages/readings/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare – 6.061 Introduction to Electric Power Systems,\nReadings<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Chapter index 9–12: synchronous, induction, DC and\npermanent-magnet machines; linked chapters not re-reviewed here. · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/13-6-electric-generators-and-back-emf\">OpenStax,\nElectric Generators and Back Emf<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · University Physics 2 §13.6:\ngenerator induction and motor back EMF; partial machine overview only. ·\nLast accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M7-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M7-L5 – Lines, Loads and Simplified Network Models<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M7 – Bachelor: Electric Power Engineering<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Three-phase\nsystems, star and delta<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Transformers\nand voltage conversion<\/a> and <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Impedances in\nsinusoidal operation<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Model a radial connection as a\nper-phase equivalent circuit, calculate voltage drop and line loss, and\nexplain which assumptions distinguish load calculations from fault\ncalculations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The radial two-bus case<\/h2>\n<p>The simplest network diagram containing a line has a sending bus and\na load bus. In a balanced three-phase system, all three identical phases\ncan be calculated using a single per-phase equivalent circuit. The\nsource has a fixed phase-voltage phasor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><munder><mi>U<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\underline U_\\mathrm S<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nEach phase of the line has a series impedance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><mi>X<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z_\\mathrm{line}=R+jX<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand the load has a phase impedance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z_\\mathrm{load}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nShunt admittance to earth is neglected in the short-line model. All\nimpedances are at the same voltage level, after conversion using an\nideal turns ratio if necessary.<\/p>\n<p>The branch current is positive from the source to the load. The\nlinear system of equations reduces to<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi>I<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msub><munder><mi>U<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><\/msub><mrow><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><munder><mi>U<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><munder><mi>U<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><munder><mi>I<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\underline I=\\frac{\\underline U_\\mathrm S}{Z_\\mathrm{line}+Z_\\mathrm{load}},\n\\qquad\n\\underline U_\\mathrm R=\\underline U_\\mathrm S-Z_\\mathrm{line}\\underline I .<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The phasor voltage drop across the line is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><munder><mi>I<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z_\\mathrm{line}\\underline I<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe difference between the voltage magnitudes also depends on the\ndirection of this phasor relative to the receiving-end voltage. For a\nsmall relative drop, it can be projected in the direction of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><munder><mi>U<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\underline U_\\mathrm R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf the current lags the load voltage by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>φ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varphi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe following approximation applies:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><munder><mi>U<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><munder><mi>U<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mi>I<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>φ<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>φ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\underline U_\\mathrm S|-|\\underline U_\\mathrm R|\n \\approx I(R\\cos\\varphi+X\\sin\\varphi).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The transverse component of the drop changes the angle. The\napproximation neglects its contribution to the magnitude at first order.\nFor a large voltage drop, the full phasors must be used.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Power loss in the three conductors<\/h2>\n<p>For three identical conductor impedances,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>R<\/mi><msup><mi>I<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_\\mathrm{loss}=3RI^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe factor of three sums the active power losses in the three line\nconductors. The quantity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>R<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the resistance of one phase in the equivalent model. An ideal\ninductive reactance consumes no active power averaged over a sinusoidal\ncycle. It changes the voltage and current, and exchanges reactive power\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>X<\/mi><msup><mi>I<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q_\\mathrm{line}=3XI^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith the rest of the network in the model.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example: increasing loss at higher current<\/h2>\n<p>A balanced model has\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>120<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_\\mathrm S=120\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nper phase,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=0.5\\,\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThree load resistors of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>11.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">11.5\\,\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\neach are connected in star. This gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=10\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>115<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_\\mathrm R=115\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>115<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>450<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_\\mathrm{load}=3\\cdot115\\cdot10\\,\\mathrm W=3\\,450\\,\\mathrm W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>150<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_\\mathrm{loss}=150\\,\\mathrm W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe supplied active power is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>600<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3\\,600\\,\\mathrm W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand satisfies the balance.<\/p>\n<p>With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5.5\\,\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nper phase in the second state,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=20\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe voltage drop is then\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the conductor loss is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>600<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">600\\,\\mathrm W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe load terminals receive\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>110<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">110\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nper phase. Doubling the current magnitude quadruples the heat dissipated\nat constant resistance.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_137_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"A linear voltage-drop graph and a quadratic power-loss graph with marked points at ten and twenty amperes.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: At fixed line resistance, increasing current from 10\nto 20 A doubles voltage drop from 5 to 10 V, while ohmic losses rise\nfourfold from 150 to 600 W.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>The graphs hold the line resistance constant and vary current as a\nparameter. In the two-bus model, the current in each case is determined\nby the load impedance. The left axis shows the difference between phase\nvoltages; the right axis shows the sum of the three resistive conductor\nlosses.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Scope and idealised fault cases<\/h2>\n<p>During a fault, a new boundary condition may replace the normal load\nmodel. An assumed ideal balanced short circuit sets the three terminal\npotentials equal in the model. The current is then determined by the\nremaining equivalent impedances, including that of the source. Zero\nimpedance in an ideal source circuit results in a singular calculation.\nUnbalanced faults require a multiphase model or decomposition into\nsymmetrical components. Arcing, dynamics, earthing, protective-device\nbehaviour and phase imbalance are not accounted for in the equivalent\nmodel considered here.<\/p>\n<p>Depending on the analysis, a constant-power model may also be\nappropriate for regulated loads. The assumption of constant complex\npower\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi>I<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mo>=<\/mo><mover><mrow><munder><mi>S<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mi>/<\/mi><msub><munder><mi>U<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><mo accent=\"true\">¯<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\underline I=\\overline{\\underline S/\\underline U_\\mathrm R}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmakes the current calculation voltage-dependent. The linear branch\nequation and this load condition give a nonlinear system of equations.\nThe modelling assumption must therefore be documented together with the\nresult.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises with solutions<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What are the power loss and load voltage in the second state of the\nexample, with the same source and twice the line resistance?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=1\\,\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>120<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>5.5<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>18.46<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=120/(1+5.5)\\,\\mathrm A\\approx18.46\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThese give\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>101.54<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_\\mathrm R\\approx101.54\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>022.49<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_\\mathrm{loss}\\approx1\\,022.49\\,\\mathrm W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Does the loss double if the resistance is doubled at the same load\nimpedance?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong> In general, the current changes as well. Here,\nsimply doubling the old loss would give\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>200<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,200\\,\\mathrm W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nRecalculating the current gives approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>022.49<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,022.49\\,\\mathrm W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Further study:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Power supply\nsystem case study<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-061-introduction-to-electric-power-systems-spring-2011/9cf8de165233601f547b284cee5c2131_MIT6_061S11_ch2.pdf\">J.\nL. Kirtley, MIT 6.061, AC Power Flow in Linear Networks<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Chapter 2,\npp. 14–15: complex power and RMS values; partial scope. · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-061-introduction-to-electric-power-systems-spring-2011/7603169f5d8514b769a46fa900b79a57_MIT6_061S11_ch4.pdf\">J.\nL. Kirtley, MIT 6.061, Introduction to Symmetrical Components<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nChapter 4, p. 2: symmetrical components for unbalanced networks; partial\nscope. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M7-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M7-L6 – Power Supply System Case Study<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M7 – Bachelor: Electric Power Engineering<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Electrical\npower balances<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Voltage\nconversion<\/a> and <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">the radial\ntwo-bus case<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Reproducibly compare two load\nstates of a balanced supply model, account for active and reactive power\nflows, and explain changes in voltage, losses and efficiency.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model and comparison question<\/h2>\n<p>The conceptual model connects an ideal three-phase supply to a\nbalanced load. An ideal transformer provides a secondary phase voltage\nof\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>120<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">120\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAll subsequent impedances are referred to the secondary side. The series\nimpedance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><mn>0.6<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z_\\mathrm{line}=(0.8+j0.6)\\,\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nper phase combines the line and passive series components. The ideal\ntransformer itself absorbs no active power.<\/p>\n<p>In state A, the load impedance is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><mn>9<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z_A=(12+j9)\\,\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nper phase. In state B, it becomes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><mn>4.5<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z_B=(6+j4.5)\\,\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nLower impedance means higher current in this comparison. The voltage\ndirectly at the load and the active power it absorbs are obtained from\nthe same current. The comparison covers two separate steady states.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Power balance using phasors<\/h2>\n<p>The constant reference is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><munder><mi>U<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>120<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\underline U_\\mathrm S=120+j0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin volts. With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z=Z_\\mathrm{line}+Z_\\mathrm{load}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe relationships are<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><munder><mi>I<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><msub><munder><mi>U<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>Z<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><munder><mi>U<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R<\/mi><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><munder><mi>I<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mo>,<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><munder><mi>S<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msub><munder><mi>U<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><\/msub><msup><munder><mi>I<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><munder><mi>S<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msub><munder><mi>U<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R<\/mi><\/msub><msup><munder><mi>I<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><munder><mi>S<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><munder><mi>I<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\underline I &amp;= \\underline U_\\mathrm S/Z,\\\\\n\\underline U_\\mathrm R &amp;= Z_\\mathrm{load}\\underline I,\\\\\n\\underline S_\\mathrm{in} &amp;= 3\\,\\underline U_\\mathrm S\\underline I^*,\\\\\n\\underline S_\\mathrm{load} &amp;= 3\\,\\underline U_\\mathrm R\\underline I^*,\\\\\n\\underline S_\\mathrm{line} &amp;= 3\\,|\\underline I|^2 Z_\\mathrm{line}.\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The quantities are RMS phasors. The supplied power is positive from\nthe source terminals into the network. Since\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><munder><mi>U<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><munder><mi>U<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><munder><mi>I<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\underline U_\\mathrm S=\\underline U_\\mathrm R+Z_\\mathrm{line}\\underline I<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe identity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><munder><mi>S<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><munder><mi>S<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><munder><mi>S<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\underline S_\\mathrm{in}=\\underline S_\\mathrm{load}+\\underline S_\\mathrm{line}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nholds. This provides a check for both active and reactive power. For\nefficiency\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>η<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta=P_\\mathrm{load}/P_\\mathrm{in}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe use only the real parts.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Reproducible model calculation<\/h2>\n<p>This Python program uses built-in complex arithmetic. The complex\npower identity is checked directly for both cases.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>u_source <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">120<\/span> <span class=\"op\">+<\/span> <span class=\"ot\">0j<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>z_line <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">0.8<\/span> <span class=\"op\">+<\/span> <span class=\"ot\">0.6j<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>cases <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> [(<span class=\"st\">&quot;A&quot;<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">12<\/span> <span class=\"op\">+<\/span> <span class=\"ot\">9j<\/span>), (<span class=\"st\">&quot;B&quot;<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">6<\/span> <span class=\"op\">+<\/span> <span class=\"ot\">4.5j<\/span>)]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> label, z_load <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> cases:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    current <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> u_source <span class=\"op\">/<\/span> (z_line <span class=\"op\">+<\/span> z_load)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    u_load <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> z_load <span class=\"op\">*<\/span> current<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    s_source <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">3<\/span> <span class=\"op\">*<\/span> u_source <span class=\"op\">*<\/span> current.conjugate()<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    s_load <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">3<\/span> <span class=\"op\">*<\/span> u_load <span class=\"op\">*<\/span> current.conjugate()<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    s_line <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">3<\/span> <span class=\"op\">*<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(current)<span class=\"op\">**<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span> <span class=\"op\">*<\/span> z_line<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(s_source <span class=\"op\">-<\/span> s_load <span class=\"op\">-<\/span> s_line) <span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">1e-9<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a href=\"#cb1-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    efficiency <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> s_load.real <span class=\"op\">/<\/span> s_source.real<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-13\"><a href=\"#cb1-13\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-14\"><a href=\"#cb1-14\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"ss\">f&quot;<\/span><span class=\"sc\">{<\/span>label<span class=\"sc\">}<\/span><span class=\"ss\">: I=<\/span><span class=\"sc\">{<\/span><span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(current)<span class=\"sc\">:.2f}<\/span><span class=\"ss\"> A, U=<\/span><span class=\"sc\">{<\/span><span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(u_load)<span class=\"sc\">:.2f}<\/span><span class=\"ss\"> V, &quot;<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-15\"><a href=\"#cb1-15\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"ss\">f&quot;Pin=<\/span><span class=\"sc\">{<\/span>s_source<span class=\"sc\">.<\/span>real<span class=\"sc\">:.2f}<\/span><span class=\"ss\"> W, Pload=<\/span><span class=\"sc\">{<\/span>s_load<span class=\"sc\">.<\/span>real<span class=\"sc\">:.2f}<\/span><span class=\"ss\"> W, &quot;<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-16\"><a href=\"#cb1-16\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"ss\">f&quot;Ploss=<\/span><span class=\"sc\">{<\/span>s_line<span class=\"sc\">.<\/span>real<span class=\"sc\">:.2f}<\/span><span class=\"ss\"> W, Qin=<\/span><span class=\"sc\">{<\/span>s_source<span class=\"sc\">.<\/span>imag<span class=\"sc\">:.2f}<\/span><span class=\"ss\"> var, &quot;<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-17\"><a href=\"#cb1-17\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"ss\">f&quot;eta=<\/span><span class=\"sc\">{<\/span>efficiency<span class=\"sc\">:.2%}<\/span><span class=\"ss\">&quot;<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-18\"><a href=\"#cb1-18\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    )<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-11\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-14\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-14\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-14\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-14\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-14\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-14\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>State<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Current magnitude<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Load voltage per phase<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Supplied active power\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_\\mathrm{in}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Load power<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Line loss<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Efficiency<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>7.50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">7.50\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>112.50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">112.50\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>160.00<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\,160.00\\,\\mathrm W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>025.00<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\,025.00\\,\\mathrm W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>135.00<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">135.00\\,\\mathrm W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>93.75<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">93.75\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>14.12<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">14.12\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>105.88<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">105.88\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>065.88<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4\\,065.88\\,\\mathrm W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>587.54<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3\\,587.54\\,\\mathrm W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>478.34<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">478.34\\,\\mathrm W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>88.24<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">88.24\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The source delivers approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>620.00<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">v<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,620.00\\,\\mathrm{var}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin A and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>049.41<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">v<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3\\,049.41\\,\\mathrm{var}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin B. In this particular comparison, the load and line impedances have\nthe same angle, so the power factor at the supply is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.8<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin both cases. Efficiency nevertheless falls because the line resistance\naccounts for a greater share of the total resistance as load impedance\ndecreases. Absolute load power increases because the higher load current\nmore than offsets the decrease in load voltage in this model.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_138_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Stacked bars for the active and reactive power of the load and line in two states, with supply totals on the right.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: Active and reactive power for two load states are\nsplit into load and line contributions; reactive line power is not an\nadditional heating loss.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>The bar lengths correspond to the unrounded calculated values; the\nfigures in the diagram are rounded to whole watts or var, respectively.\nThe active and reactive power scales are separate. The orange reactive\npower portion represents the reactive power exchanged by the line, not\nadditional heat dissipation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limitations and further exercise<\/h2>\n<p>The calculation fixes source voltage, frequency and impedances. Load\ncharacteristics of electrical machines and regulated constant-power\nloads require their own load models. A detailed load-flow analysis may\ninclude multiple buses, modelled generator conditions, parallel paths\nand voltage-dependent loads. The example calculation can be used to\nderive voltage deviations and proportions of losses within this\nequivalent model.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Exercise 1<\/strong> Recalculate state B with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1.2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><mn>0.9<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z_\\mathrm{line}=(1.2+j0.9)\\,\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong> The magnitude of the total impedance is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>9<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">9\\,\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>13.33<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=13.33\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_\\mathrm R=100\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>200<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_\\mathrm{load}=3\\,200\\,\\mathrm W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>640<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_\\mathrm{loss}=640\\,\\mathrm W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe balance gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>840<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_\\mathrm{in}=3\\,840\\,\\mathrm W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>η<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>83.33<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta=83.33\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Exercise 2<\/strong> Why is the current magnitude used in the\nloss formula for the line?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi>I<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><msup><munder><mi>I<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><munder><mi>I<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\underline I\\underline I^*=|\\underline I|^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis real and independent of the phase reference. The real part of the\nline impedance gives the positive resistive loss, and its imaginary part\ngives the line’s reactive power.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Further study:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;page=study-path&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Study\npathway with the next applied project<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-061-introduction-to-electric-power-systems-spring-2011/9cf8de165233601f547b284cee5c2131_MIT6_061S11_ch2.pdf\">J.\nL. Kirtley, MIT 6.061, AC Power Flow in Linear Networks<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Chapter 2,\npp. 14–15: real, reactive and apparent power; partial scope. · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-061-introduction-to-electric-power-systems-spring-2011/2f9042dd664b5bd07e4b5d4cb4930062_MIT6_061S11_ch5.pdf\">J.\nL. Kirtley, MIT 6.061, Introduction to Load Flow<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Chapter 5, § 2,\np. 1: bus voltages and complex power balance; partial scope. · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
